      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "WDrxRkYUSvnkIgQJUJ6U2",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1238.39990234375,
      "y": 251.10000610351562,
      "width": 180.0762565929559,
      "height": 157.6000671386718,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ao",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 579096820,
      "version": 328,
      "versionNonce": 1665017174,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789681904553,
      "created": 1789657564916,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> 500M users\n-> 200M daily users\n-> 100 tweet reads \nper day (20 Billion)\n-> 1 MB per tweet\n-> 20 PB of Data\n-> 50M new tweets a\nday",
      "fontSize": 15.76000671386718,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> 500M users\n-> 200M daily users\n-> 100 tweet reads \nper day (20 Billion)\n-> 1 MB per tweet\n-> 20 PB of Data\n-> 50M new tweets a day",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "k7hp_3jfPqcufxKzUrBKx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 838,
      "y": 260.5,
      "width": 286.1998291015625,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aq",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 806733388,
      "version": 104,
      "versionNonce": 320996106,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789681904553,
      "created": 1789657688804,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> Eventual Consistency\n-> Should support fast reads\n-> Highly available\n-> Low Latency",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> Eventual Consistency\n-> Should support fast reads\n-> Highly available\n-> Low Latency",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "SLf6NtTVdzHmzSA9dDD5h",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -64.6005249023442,
      "y": 649.5001169840496,
      "width": 2588.40234375,
      "height": 385,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "ar",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1430895860,
      "version": 1831,
      "versionNonce": 708512406,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1789681904553,
      "created": 1789657711543,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> Reads are much more frequent than writes, so we can use a distributed NoSQL database for high-scale read/write workloads instead of relying entirely on a relational database.\n-> Tweets can contain images or videos, with an average media size of around 1 MB per tweet.\n-> Media files can be stored in object/blob storage such as Amazon S3 and served through a CDN for faster delivery and reduced load on application servers.\n-> We can maintain a Follow/Followee store that maps users to their followers and the users they follow, allowing us to efficiently retrieve follower relationships.\n-> Since this is a read-heavy system, we can use read replicas to distribute read traffic and reduce the load on the primary database.\n-> We can shard the tweet database based on a suitable partition key such as User ID or Tweet ID, allowing us to distribute data and traffic across multiple database nodes.\n-> Frequently accessed data, such as recent tweets, popular tweets, and user timelines, can be stored in a Redis cache to provide low-latency reads.\n-> Sharding and caching help reduce database load and improve overall latency by distributing traffic and serving frequently accessed data from memory.\n-> To further improve latency, we can generate user timelines asynchronously using background workers rather than building the entire feed synchronously when the user requests it.\n-> We can implement fan-out on write for normal users, where a newly created tweet is asynchronously pushed as a tweet ID into the precomputed Redis timelines of the user's followers.\n-> For users with a very large number of followers, fan-out on write can become expensive, so we can use fan-out on read and retrieve those users' recent tweets when generating the feed.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> Reads are much more frequent than writes, so we can use a distributed NoSQL database for high-scale read/write workloads instead of relying entirely on a relational database.\n-> Tweets can contain images or videos, with an average media size of around 1 MB per tweet.\n-> Media files can be stored in object/blob storage such as Amazon S3 and served through a CDN for faster delivery and reduced load on application servers.\n-> We can maintain a Follow/Followee store that maps users to their followers and the users they follow, allowing us to efficiently retrieve follower relationships.\n-> Since this is a read-heavy system, we can use read replicas to distribute read traffic and reduce the load on the primary database.\n-> We can shard the tweet database based on a suitable partition key such as User ID or Tweet ID, allowing us to distribute data and traffic across multiple database nodes.\n-> Frequently accessed data, such as recent tweets, popular tweets, and user timelines, can be stored in a Redis cache to provide low-latency reads.\n-> Sharding and caching help reduce database load and improve overall latency by distributing traffic and serving frequently accessed data from memory.\n-> To further improve latency, we can generate user timelines asynchronously using background workers rather than building the entire feed synchronously when the user requests it.\n-> We can implement fan-out on write for normal users, where a newly created tweet is asynchronously pushed as a tweet ID into the precomputed Redis timelines of the user's followers.\n-> For users with a very large number of followers, fan-out on write can become expensive, so we can use fan-out on read and retrieve those users' recent tweets when generating the feed.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
