    {
      "id": "5RqGE1SX6noULmD91hu4a",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1195.6000366210938,
      "y": 252.10000610351562,
      "width": 129.29991149902344,
      "height": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 980986762,
      "version": 106,
      "versionNonce": 1292520022,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789682439217,
      "created": 1789682421859,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "->100M users\neveryday\n-> 50B msgs\neveryday",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "->100M users\neveryday\n-> 50B msgs\neveryday",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "bqa2CGbo9A9ItcGI9-Qyu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 241.37160818917425,
      "y": 394.15709141322554,
      "width": 1243.359130859375,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0c",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1352042006,
      "version": 1872,
      "versionNonce": 556623626,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1789683959557,
      "created": 1789682443430,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "-> Messages are small, so connection management is more important than raw message storage.\n-> WebSockets can be used to maintain long-lived connections between clients and the messaging servers.\n-> When A sends a message to B, the Sessions Service can determine B's active connection and notify A once the message \nhas been successfully delivered to B.\n-> When B receives and reads the message, the client sends a read acknowledgment back through the Sessions Service, \nwhich then notifies A. This supports Sent, Delivered, and Read receipts.\n-> Whenever a user performs an activity, such as opening the app or reading a message, we can treat it as an activity \nevent and update the user's latest activity timestamp.\n-> When B wants to know A's last-seen status, we can retrieve A's latest activity timestamp from the database.\n-> For users who are offline, messages should be persisted in the server-side Message DB and delivered when the user \nreconnects, with retries until delivery is acknowledged.\n-> We can have a decoupled Group Service connected to a Group DB that maintains the members of each group.\n-> When A sends a message to a group, the Group Service retrieves the group member IDs, and the delivery workers \nuse the Sessions Service to route the message to each member's active connection.\n-> WhatsApp groups can support up to 200 members in our assumed system requirements.\n-> To reduce processing load on the WebSocket Gateways, we can introduce a Message/Parser Service that handles \ntasks such as validation, parsing, and converting incoming messages into a standardized format.\n-> We can place Kafka or another message queue in front of the Group/Message processing services so that \nmessages are buffered during service failures and can be retried when the service becomes available again.\n-> The Group DB can be partitioned by Group ID to distribute the data and efficiently retrieve group membership information.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "-> Messages are small, so connection management is more important than raw message storage.\n-> WebSockets can be used to maintain long-lived connections between clients and the messaging servers.\n-> When A sends a message to B, the Sessions Service can determine B's active connection and notify A once the message \nhas been successfully delivered to B.\n-> When B receives and reads the message, the client sends a read acknowledgment back through the Sessions Service, \nwhich then notifies A. This supports Sent, Delivered, and Read receipts.\n-> Whenever a user performs an activity, such as opening the app or reading a message, we can treat it as an activity \nevent and update the user's latest activity timestamp.\n-> When B wants to know A's last-seen status, we can retrieve A's latest activity timestamp from the database.\n-> For users who are offline, messages should be persisted in the server-side Message DB and delivered when the user \nreconnects, with retries until delivery is acknowledged.\n-> We can have a decoupled Group Service connected to a Group DB that maintains the members of each group.\n-> When A sends a message to a group, the Group Service retrieves the group member IDs, and the delivery workers \nuse the Sessions Service to route the message to each member's active connection.\n-> WhatsApp groups can support up to 200 members in our assumed system requirements.\n-> To reduce processing load on the WebSocket Gateways, we can introduce a Message/Parser Service that handles \ntasks such as validation, parsing, and converting incoming messages into a standardized format.\n-> We can place Kafka or another message queue in front of the Group/Message processing services so that \nmessages are buffered during service failures and can be retried when the service becomes available again.\n-> The Group DB can be partitioned by Group ID to distribute the data and efficiently retrieve group membership information.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
